Why DevOps Engineers Must Understand Networking

You might not be a network engineer, but if you’re in DevOps, you absolutely need to understand how networks work. Why? Because you're managing traffic, configuring security, deploying services — and when something breaks in production, it's often the network.

🧠 What DevOps Engineers Need to Know About Networking:
✅ IP Addressing
 Understand public vs private IPs, CIDR blocks, and subnetting.
✅ Ports & Protocols
 Know what runs on ports like 22 (SSH), 80 (HTTP), 443 (HTTPS), 3306 (MySQL), etc.
✅ DNS
 What’s really happening when you type a URL? Learn how DNS resolution works.
✅ Firewalls & Security Groups
 Control traffic in and out of your services — essential in AWS, Azure, and GCP.
✅ Load Balancers
 Distribute traffic and avoid single points of failure.
✅ VPCs & Subnets
 Master VPC design and subnet isolation to keep services secure and scalable.
💡 Real-life example: Your app isn’t reachable from the internet. It might not be the code — it could be:
A closed port
A misconfigured security group
A bad DNS record
A subnet route issue
📣 Pro Tip: Use nmap, netstat, and cloud-native tools to troubleshoot traffic and access issues.
